    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay shows how the process of colonialism moves from the physical body to the mind and the soul, inflicting\r\nviolence along the way. Through an analysis of Jordan Peele’s movie \"Get Out,\" Jaeden Clark illustrates this process and argues that decolonization should follow the same linear track, but backwards, starting with the liberation of the soul and then the body.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Clark, J. (2024). From the Body, to the Mind, to the Soul: The Dialectical Disfigurement of the Self. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/xy965mf5303. https://doi.org/10.25740/xy965mf5303.",
